WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.569
This is a simulated robot installation:
one robot arm at a kitchen table.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:10.400
Its policy, the model that turns camera
images into motion, is fixed.

3
00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.277
Agents watch the installation, run
experiments on it,

4
00:00:14.277 --> 00:00:17.915
and change how the installation uses that
policy.

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.708
First story: make this one job.

6
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.273
The robot already has skills.

7
00:00:25.273 --> 00:00:30.538
Each skill is one task on this table, done
by the policy under its own instruction.

8
00:00:30.538 --> 00:00:33.365
Here, each one runs alone and delivers.

9
00:00:33.365 --> 00:00:36.746
The policy does not change anywhere in
this film.

10
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.771
A shift lead asks for one job: the cheese
into the black bowl,

11
00:00:41.771 --> 00:00:44.437
and the loaded bowl onto the cabinet top.

12
00:00:44.437 --> 00:00:47.102
The cheese goes in before the bowl moves.

13
00:00:47.102 --> 00:00:49.063
The agent proposes the bar:

14
00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:53.438
the job must deliver on at least 14 of 20
fresh starts it was never run on.

15
00:00:53.438 --> 00:00:56.858
The lead approves that bar before any
program is tested.

16
00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.914
The obvious chain switches to the next
skill the moment the first one reports

17
00:01:02.914 --> 00:01:03.985
success.

18
00:01:03.985 --> 00:01:06.876
That is the signal published scores count.

19
00:01:06.876 --> 00:01:10.355
On the first 10 development starts, it
delivers on 0.

20
00:01:10.355 --> 00:01:12.871
A success signal is not a delivery.

21
00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:18.925
The agent reads the object traces, looks
at the frames, and writes its own check:

22
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:22.673
was the cheese released and settled when
the bowl task began,

23
00:01:22.673 --> 00:01:25.292
and did it stay in the bowl to the end?

24
00:01:25.292 --> 00:01:28.270
Then it tests transitions on the
installation.

25
00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.097
Letting the cheese instruction run on a
little after its goal first holds delivers

26
00:01:33.097 --> 00:01:35.099
on 9 of the same 10 starts.

27
00:01:35.099 --> 00:01:38.385
On 20 fresh starts, the same program
delivers on 11.

28
00:01:38.385 --> 00:01:42.339
Adding a return home at the end breaks
starts that had delivered.

29
00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:48.203
The result is short of the bar, so the
agent does not release the job.

30
00:01:48.203 --> 00:01:52.922
It writes its account to the shift lead:
the best fresh result, 11 of 20,

31
00:01:52.922 --> 00:01:57.753
against the agreed 14 of 20, and a
finishing change that made things worse.

32
00:01:57.753 --> 00:02:01.251
It saves its checks and a skill for the
next shift.

33
00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.266
A second shift takes the same request,

34
00:02:05.266 --> 00:02:08.905
with the first shift's checks and skill in
its library.

35
00:02:08.905 --> 00:02:13.414
It runs the first shift's check on its own
baseline before adapting it.

36
00:02:13.414 --> 00:02:16.781
A longer continuation delivers on 17 of 20
starts.

37
00:02:16.781 --> 00:02:19.497
Those starts had been examined before,

38
00:02:19.497 --> 00:02:22.919
so the agent does not count them as fresh
evidence,

39
00:02:22.919 --> 00:02:25.689
and it stops without a release as well.

40
00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:28.894
A third shift starts from the second's
library.

41
00:02:28.894 --> 00:02:29.926
For it,

42
00:02:29.926 --> 00:02:34.869
the lead adds that a release which misses
on the reserved starts is acceptable.

43
00:02:34.869 --> 00:02:38.997
Its shorter continuation delivers on 9 of
10 development starts,

44
00:02:38.997 --> 00:02:41.387
and the agent saves it as a job.

45
00:02:41.387 --> 00:02:43.777
The first shift took 5 sessions,

46
00:02:43.777 --> 00:02:48.448
100 simulated starts and 38 minutes of the
agent's model time; the second,

47
00:02:48.448 --> 00:02:52.414
5 sessions, 130 starts and 45 minutes; the
third, 5 sessions,

48
00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:54.423
60 starts and 39 minutes.

49
00:02:55.700 --> 00:03:00.713
The saved job runs on the 20 reserved
starts, which no shift had touched,

50
00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:04.016
and the naive chain runs on the same
starts.

51
00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:05.962
The lead's bar is 14.

52
00:03:05.962 --> 00:03:09.324
The job delivers on 13; the naive chain on
1.

53
00:03:09.324 --> 00:03:10.739
Rescued: 13.

54
00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:12.037
Broken: 1.

55
00:03:12.037 --> 00:03:13.924
Order violations: 0.

56
00:03:13.924 --> 00:03:17.109
Short of the bar, the job is not released.

57
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:21.378
The robot policy stayed the same.

58
00:03:21.378 --> 00:03:23.280
The job is not released.

59
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.610
The installation's library now holds the
saved job,

60
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:31.260
4 checks and the skills of every shift,
and the next shift starts from them.

61
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:51.901
Second story: make that a check the next
shift can run.

62
00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.418
A fleet of robot arms works at several
sites.

63
00:03:56.418 --> 00:04:00.427
At one site, inference stays in the
customer's data center,

64
00:04:00.427 --> 00:04:04.379
so commands arrive late, and nobody on
site can undo that.

65
00:04:04.379 --> 00:04:09.630
Task telemetry shows a lag there, and a
lag on one robot at another site as well.

66
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:14.655
From the telemetry alone, the agent cannot
tell what kind of lag each one is.

67
00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.348
So the agent builds a measurement.

68
00:04:18.348 --> 00:04:21.841
From a bounded set of motions it composes
a step probe,

69
00:04:21.841 --> 00:04:26.064
and asks for it on a robot at the delayed
site, on the lagging robot,

70
00:04:26.064 --> 00:04:29.609
on a healthy reference and on a robot at
the third site.

71
00:04:29.609 --> 00:04:32.737
Commanded against measured: at the delayed
site,

72
00:04:32.737 --> 00:04:35.969
the arm starts late by the same amount
every time.

73
00:04:35.969 --> 00:04:40.140
On the lagging robot, the delay changes
from one repeat to the next,

74
00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:42.173
and grows within the probe.

75
00:04:42.173 --> 00:04:43.893
One is a fixed delay.

76
00:04:43.893 --> 00:04:45.718
The other is a backlog.

77
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.478
The probe picks candidates; task
experiments on paired starts decide.

78
00:04:51.478 --> 00:04:54.961
The agent adopts its changes, each with
its reason:

79
00:04:54.961 --> 00:04:59.218
compensate for the delay at the first site
rather than remove it,

80
00:04:59.218 --> 00:05:02.535
roll back the software update at the third
site,

81
00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:05.576
and service the lagging robot's
controller.

82
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:37.243
Then the changed fleet runs 576 reserved
attempts the agent never saw,

83
00:05:37.243 --> 00:05:41.268
beside the original state on the same
attempts.

84
00:05:41.268 --> 00:05:43.928
Before: 451 of 576 succeed.

85
00:05:43.928 --> 00:05:45.497
After: 558.

86
00:05:45.497 --> 00:05:47.203
Rescued: 113.

87
00:05:47.203 --> 00:05:48.703
Broken: 6.

88
00:05:48.703 --> 00:05:52.455
The lagging robot goes from 29 to 46 of
48.

89
00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:59.161
The probe and its analysis became a recipe
in the library, runnable on named robots.

90
00:05:59.161 --> 00:06:03.050
On a later fleet, robots fail again, and
the recipe runs.

91
00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:08.010
At the third site, the arms first move at
row 18 and are half-way at row 21:

92
00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.476
a fixed delay.

93
00:06:09.476 --> 00:06:13.985
The robot that fails on its own responds
like the healthy reference.

94
00:06:13.985 --> 00:06:16.127
Its first movement: row 1.

95
00:06:16.127 --> 00:06:17.987
The reference: row 1.

96
00:06:17.987 --> 00:06:20.975
So the skill's rule points at the images,

97
00:06:20.975 --> 00:06:24.864
not at the controller service that fixed
the first fleet.

98
00:06:24.864 --> 00:06:29.542
The operator's recorded decision follows
it: no service for that robot;

99
00:06:29.542 --> 00:06:32.360
inspect its images and its change log,

100
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:37.264
and test the first fleet's adaptation at
the third site before adopting it.

101
00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.058
Every clip in this film is a recorded run.

102
00:06:41.058 --> 00:06:42.822
The record lists each one.
